# SRG-APP-000086-AU-000030  -  CAT III
# Time stamps recorded on the log records in the Central Log Server must be
# configured to synchronize to within one second of the host server or, if
# NTP is configured directly in the log server, the NTP time source must be
# the same as the host and devices within its scope of coverage.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000086-AU-000030
group:           V-206450
severity:        low
cat:             CAT III
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-000174
control_rev5:    AU-12

contribution:    Splunk ships no NTP client, so it cannot synchronize a clock; what it
                 configures is faithful timestamp recognition on the records it receives,
                 plus the skew bounds it tolerates between a search head and its peers.

governs:         limits.conf - Limits on how much work searches and data processing may consume.
governs:         props.conf - How incoming data is recognized, parsed and timestamped.

check:           props.conf [<spec>] DATETIME_CONFIG not_in NONE|CURRENT
check:           limits.conf [search] max_tolerable_skew max 60
check:           limits.conf [search] addpeer_skew_limit max 600

external:        Clock synchronization to within one second of the host server, and to the
                 same upstream time source as the covered hosts and devices, is performed
                 by the operating system's time service; Splunk Enterprise 10.4.2 contains
                 no NTP client for the check text's conditional clause to apply to.

citation:        Under-test corrected. The original excluded only NONE. props.conf.spec
                 treats the two values together - "Both 'CURRENT' and 'NONE' explicitly
                 disable the per-text timestamp identification" - and CURRENT is the worse
                 case for this requirement, since it substitutes the aggregator's clock for
                 the time the record carries, which is exactly the agreement the
                 requirement is about. SRG-APP-000096-AU-000690 in this same group already
                 excludes both, so leaving CURRENT permitted here made two criteria resting
                 on one setting disagree. The skew conditions are kept: both are ceilings
                 and the direction is correct.
finding:         Timestamp recognition is disabled for the sourcetypes in scope
                 (DATETIME_CONFIG set to NONE or CURRENT), so the time recorded on a
                 received record is the log server's own processing time rather than the
                 time the record carries.
